## Experimental Section
## Chemicals and characterization
NMR spectra were recorded on a Brüker DPX-400/500 spectrometer at 400/500 MHz for $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR and 100/125 MHz for $^{13}{\mathsf C}$ NMR. Data for $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR are recorded as follows: chemical shift (ppm), multiplicity (s, singlet; d, doublet; t, triplet; q, quarter; $\mathsf{m},$ , multiplet), coupling constant $(\mathsf{H}z)$ , integration. Data for $^{13}{\mathsf C}$ NMR are reported in terms of chemical shift (δ, ppm). Mass spectra were determined on a Esquire-LC-00075 mass spectrometer. All infrared experiments were performed on a Brüker Alpha FT-IR spectrometer using 1 mg of the solid sample at a $4~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ resolution. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data were collected at ambient temperature on a Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer at $40~\mathsf{k V},$ , $40~\mathsf{m A}$ for Cu Kα $(\lambda=1.5418\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ), with a scan speed of 1 sec/step and a 2θ range of 5-40º. The Brunauer−Emmer−Teller (BET) surface areas were measured by Micromeritics ASAP 2020 analyzer at $77~\mathsf{K}.$ . Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) data was collected at Perkin Elmer TGA 7 running from $50~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to ${500}^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ with a scan rate of $10\ ^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{m i n}}$ . Analyses of the morphology and chemical composition of the samples were conducted by a Hitachi S4800 field emission scanning electron mi-croscope (FE-SEM). The computational modeling for the topological characteristic of the host, monomers, and linear polymers were archived by Discovery Studio 2.5 program. Gel permeation chromatography (GPC) analysis was performed on Waters 1525 with refractive index (RI) detector. Starting materials and solvents were purchased and used without further purification from commercial suppliers (Sigma-Aldrich, Alfa Aesar, TCI, and others).
## Conclusion
In this study, highly porous chiral poymers/MOF composites were prepared by a rational design, featuring a strategy involving the in situ polymerization of pre-impregnated chiral monomers within MOFs. The chiral catalytic sites attached
The synthesis of MIL-101-PP1 composites.
A solution of compound S1 (300mg, 0.86 mmol), methyl acrylate (4.3 mmol, as co-polymeric monomer) and 2,2- azobisisobutyronitrile (AIBN, $42.3{\mathrm{~}}_{\mathsf{m g}},$ $0.26\mathrm{\mmol}$ , as radical initiator) in toluene $(2\mathsf{m L})$ was impregnated into Cr-MIL-101 (1 g), and the mixture was allowed to stand under nitrogen atmosphere at $4^{\circ}C$ for 12 hours to reach distribution equilibrium of the monomers through the cavities of MIL-101. The polymerization was conducted under nitrogen atmosphere at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 5 days. Then, the obtained powder was soaked in DMF for 3 days to completely remove all unreacted monomer and loosely attached polymers between MIL-101 particles. DMF was then removed by washing with ${\mathsf{C H}}_{2}{\mathsf{C l}}_{2}$ . Finally, the desired polymer MIL-101-PP1 was obtained by further deprotection of N-Boc group in mixture of $C H_{2}C l_{2}$ $(4~\mathsf{m L})$ and ${\mathsf{C F}}_{3}{\mathsf{C O}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}$ $(1~\mathsf{m L})$ for 4 hours at room temperature, and was successively washed with $C H_{2}C l_{2}$ and dried at $80~^{\circ}C$ overnight.
## The extraction of linear polymers PP1 from MIL-101-PP1 composites
Prior to deprotection of N-Boc, the MIL-101-PP1 composites $(200~\mathrm{mg})$ was fully digested using $10\ \mathrm{wt}.\%$ sodium hydroxide aqueous solution $(0.8~\mathsf{m L})$ , then the mixture was acidified to neutral condition $(\mathsf{P H}\sim7)$ using 6N hydrochloric acid. With aim to precipitate the terephthalic acid and salts (e.g. NaCl, $\mathsf{C r C l}_{3})$ , acetone $(50~\mathrm{\mL})$ was added, and the brownish solution was obtained by filtration. Then, the extracted mixture was concentrated in vacuum to afford PP1-Boc. Successively, the PP1-Boc was added $C H_{2}C l_{2}$ $(4~\mathsf{m L})$ and ${\mathsf{C F}}_{3}{\mathsf{C O}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}$ $\smash{\{1\ m L\}}$ ) and stirred for 4 hours at room temperature to completely remove N-Boc group, and the desired linear polymers PP1 were obtained by evaporation of the reaction solvent.
