## Typical reaction procedure for Aldol reaction
A suspension of the aldehyde $(0.1~\mathrm{mmol})$ and cyclohexanone $(0.1~\mathsf{m L})$ and corresponding chiral catalyst (0.005 mmol, 17.8 mg for MIL-101-PP1) was stirred at room temperature for indicated time (Table 2). The reaction was quenched by adding ethyl acetate. Upon filtration, the catalyst was washed with ${\mathsf{C H}}_{2}{\mathsf{C l}}_{2}$ . The organic layers were collected and concentrated in vacuum. The crude product was checked by $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectroscopy to give reaction yield and diastereoselectivity (d.r. values) using dibromomethane as an internal standard, and was then purified by chromatography (petroleum ether/ethyl acetate) to afford desired product. The ee value was determined by chiral stationary phase HPLC analysis.
